# Testing

## Write tests from requirements

Tests encode the intended behavior (spec, design notes, player-visible rules), not the current implementation.

```cpp
// ❌ BAD — locks in whatever the code does today
CHECK(path.tiles.size() == impl.CachedHopCount());

 // ✅ GOOD — asserts the requirement (shortest wrap path is one step)
CHECK(path.tiles.size() == 1);
CHECK(path.tiles.front() == &rDestAcrossSeam);
```

When behavior changes, update tests only if the **requirement** changed. Do not weaken or relocate assertions just to make a green suite after an implementation change.

## Do not test shipping config

Tests must not read `config/` and assert what it contains. Shipping numbers, ids, and flags are free to change without a test edit.

When a case needs a specific value, put that value in a fixture the test owns: a file under `tests/fixtures/`, or a config object or temp file the test builds. Assert against the fixture.

```cpp
// ❌ BAD — fails when decoration.json changes
const auto config = parser.ParseConfig("config/worldGen/decoration.json");
CHECK(config.moisture.aridThreshold == Catch::Approx(0.4f));

// ✅ GOOD — the fixture owns the number
const auto config = parser.ParseConfig(FixturePath("decoration.json"));
CHECK(config.moisture.aridThreshold == Catch::Approx(0.4f));
```

A test that only checks "the shipping file loads" is still testing shipping config. Cover the parser or the behavior with a fixture instead.

## When a test fails after a change

Decide which is true before editing the test:

1. **Requirements changed** — The product/spec now expects different behavior. Update or replace the test to match the new requirements, and note that in the summary.
2. **Implementation changed (or regressed)** — The requirement is unchanged. The failure is a bug signal: investigate and fix the code (or explain why the requirement was wrong). Do **not** delete, skip, or dodge the assertion to silence it.

```text
# ❌ BAD
Test failed after adding wrap → move the case off the seam / drop the check

# ✅ GOOD
Test failed after adding wrap → either assert the new wrap requirement, or fix the bug
```

## Always report failures

In the final summary for the user, **always mention test failures** when any occurred during the work — names or filters, what failed, and whether you treated them as a requirement change or an implementation bug — even if everything passes by the end of the session.
